### Implement Custom Health Indicators in Spring Boot

Source: https://context7.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/llms.txt

Creates custom health indicators that implement HealthIndicator or ReactiveHealthIndicator to check application dependencies like external services, databases, and disk space. Health status is exposed via the /actuator/health endpoint and returns UP or DOWN status with optional details.

```java
import org.springframework.boot.health.contributor.Health;
import org.springframework.boot.health.contributor.HealthIndicator;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class MyHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {

    private final ExternalService externalService;

    public MyHealthIndicator(ExternalService externalService) {
        this.externalService = externalService;
    }

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        int errorCode = check();
        if (errorCode != 0) {
            return Health.down()
                .withDetail("Error Code", errorCode)
                .withDetail("Description", "Service unavailable")
                .build();
        }
        return Health.up()
            .withDetail("Service", "External API")
            .withDetail("Status", "Available")
            .build();
    }

    private int check() {
        try {
            externalService.ping();
            return 0;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return 1;
        }
    }
}
```

--------------------------------

### GET /actuator/health

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/blob/main/documentation/spring-boot-actuator-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/api/partials/nav-actuator-rest-api.adoc

Returns the health status of the application and its components. This endpoint provides detailed information about the application's operational state and any dependent services.

```APIDOC
## GET /actuator/health

### Description
Returns the health status of the application and its components including database, disk space, and other health indicators.

### Method
GET

### Endpoint
/actuator/health

### Response
#### Success Response (200)
- **status** (string) - Overall health status (UP, DOWN, OUT_OF_SERVICE, UNKNOWN)
- **components** (object) - Individual component health statuses

#### Response Example
{
  "status": "UP",
  "components": {
    "db": {
      "status": "UP",
      "details": {
        "database": "PostgreSQL",
        "validationQuery": "isValid()"
      }
    },
    "diskSpace": {
      "status": "UP",
      "details": {
        "total": 1099511627776,
        "free": 549755813888,
        "threshold": 10485760,
        "exists": true
      }
    }
  }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Implement Reactive Health Indicators in Spring Boot

Source: https://context7.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/llms.txt

Creates reactive health indicators using ReactiveHealthIndicator interface with Mono return types for non-blocking health checks. Useful for checking async operations and external services in reactive applications.

```java
@Component
public class MyReactiveHealthIndicator implements ReactiveHealthIndicator {

    @Override
    public Mono<Health> health() {
        return checkHealth()
            .map(status -> Health.up().withDetail("status", status).build())
            .onErrorResume(ex -> Mono.just(Health.down(ex).build()));
    }

    private Mono<String> checkHealth() {
        return Mono.just("OK");
    }
}
```

### Writing Custom HealthIndicators > Implementation Basics

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/blob/main/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/pages/actuator/endpoints.adoc

To provide custom health information, you can register Spring beans that implement the HealthIndicator interface. You need to provide an implementation of the `health()` method and return a Health response. The Health response should include a status and can optionally include additional details to be displayed. The identifier for a given HealthIndicator is the name of the bean without the HealthIndicator suffix, if it exists.

--------------------------------

### Health Information > Health Contributors

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/blob/main/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/pages/actuator/endpoints.adoc

Health information is collected from the content of a `HealthContributorRegistry`, which by default contains all `HealthContributor` instances defined in the application context. Spring Boot includes a number of auto-configured `HealthContributor` beans, and developers can also write their own. A `HealthContributor` can be either a `HealthIndicator` or a `CompositeHealthContributor`, allowing for flexible composition of health checks across different components of an application.

